      <section data-sec="" id="before" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px 44px;">
        <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: var(--vy-ink);">Before: three systems and a month-end ritual</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Distribution ran through a channel manager, which did its job. Money ran through a spreadsheet per owner, rebuilt by hand every month from booking exports, bank statements and whatever receipts had made it back from the islands. Cleaning ran through a WhatsApp group, which worked until roughly the twentieth villa and then quietly stopped.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">The month-end was the part that hurt. Four days of assembling thirty-one statements, followed by two weeks of owners phoning to ask what a line meant. Not because the numbers were wrong — they were usually right — but because a figure in a spreadsheet cell is a claim with no evidence attached.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-sec="" id="switch" style="max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px 44px;">
        <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: var(--vy-ink);">The switch</h2>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">They moved in early May, deliberately: bookings on the books for July and August, but a quiet fortnight to work in. Reservations and two years of owner statements imported in the first week, with a reconciliation sheet that found nine bookings where the spreadsheet and the channel manager disagreed. All nine were spreadsheet transcription errors, none material, all quietly corrected.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">Week three was parallel running. The cleaning team worked the board and the WhatsApp group at once, which everybody found irritating and which caught two properties whose changeover times had been wrong in the import. Channels cut over on a Tuesday in the fourth week; the fifth was owner onboarding, which took longer than expected because owners had questions once they could actually see things.</p>
        <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: var(--vy-muted);">No bookings were lost. One rate plan for a single villa was mapped to the wrong season and was caught by the sample-pricing check before it sold anything.</p>
      </section>

      <section data-sec="" id="after" data-nopad="" style="position: relative; overflow: hidden; margin: 0 0 44px; padding: 66px 0 70px; background: linear-gradient(165deg,var(--vy-dark) 0%,#2A211B 52%,#221A15 100%);">
        <div style="position: absolute; top: -120px; left: 8%; width: 400px; height: 400px; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--vy-accent-3); filter: blur(90px); opacity: .26; pointer-events: none;"></div>
        <div style="position: relative; max-width: 780px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 28px;">
          <h2 style="margin: 0 0 16px; font-family: var(--vy-display); font-weight: 600; font-size: 28px; letter-spacing: -.03em; color: #fff;">After: the phone stopped ringing</h2>
          <p style="margin: 0 0 18px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: rgba(255,247,240,.78);">The month-end assembly went from four days to an afternoon of review, because the statements build themselves from bookings and expenses that were already recorded. The larger change was what happened afterwards: owners with a portal stopped calling, because every question they used to ask is answered on a page they can open at midnight from another country.</p>
          <p style="margin: 0 0 26px; font-size: 16.5px; line-height: 1.76; color: rgba(255,247,240,.78);">Housekeeping moved onto the board and the group chat became social again. The unexpected benefit was the maintenance side: a ticket that takes a villa off sale until it is fixed removed a class of problem nobody had been counting, where a house with a broken air conditioner kept selling because the person who knew was not the person who managed the calendar.</p>
          <div data-two="" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 14px;">
